WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:04.650
It's great to welcome to the program today,
Lewis Raven Wallace, author and creator of

00:00:04.650 --> 00:00:09.650
The View From Somewhere.
A book and podcast about the myth of objectivity

00:00:09.650 --> 00:00:15.929
in journalism. Also, co-founder of Press on
Southern Media Collective. Thanks for your

00:00:15.929 --> 00:00:20.449
time during during these strange times. I
guess I would say.

00:00:20.449 --> 00:00:24.930
Hey, yeah.
Or so to begin with. I will sometimes get

00:00:24.930 --> 00:00:33.010
these beautiful emails from people in my audience
who say, David, I love that you are not biased

00:00:33.010 --> 00:00:39.180
in your reporting. And while I will gladly
accept any compliment, it concerns me a little

00:00:39.180 --> 00:00:45.280
bit from a media literacy perspective because
I do an opinion program. I am not doing reporting.

00:00:45.280 --> 00:00:50.440
I'm doing commentary. And the program is very
much biased in the sense that it is my opinion

00:00:50.440 --> 00:00:56.710
and my opinion is certainly partisan. So can
you start to talk about some of the categorization

00:00:56.710 --> 00:01:03.250
of media that needs to be done and that maybe
not everybody is doing as well as they should?

00:01:03.250 --> 00:01:10.600
Yeah. That's such a cool and weird anecdote
that really sounds familiar to me in that

00:01:10.600 --> 00:01:16.240
I think there's been this sort of bending
of the concept of media bias over time to

00:01:16.240 --> 00:01:22.930
the point that, you know, a lot of us think
that biased media is just anything we don't

00:01:22.930 --> 00:01:30.350
like. Right. Or don't agree with or that doesn't
like vibe with us. Yes. And and so I you know,

00:01:30.350 --> 00:01:37.690
a part of my sort of thesis has been to just
get rid of this idea that media bias itself

00:01:37.690 --> 00:01:42.729
is the problem and accept that all media in
some way is biased.

00:01:42.729 --> 00:01:49.479
Right. We have a frame, we have an ideology.
We have not, you know, not necessarily a partisan

00:01:49.479 --> 00:01:55.300
political stance, but often that, too, that
we're bringing to anything that we produce

00:01:55.300 --> 00:02:01.369
as as journalists. And so it's less about
trying to be unbiased and more about doing

00:02:01.369 --> 00:02:08.160
a really rigorous investigation of what our
own biases and perspectives are and being

00:02:08.160 --> 00:02:13.970
transparent about those. And so it sounds
like, you know, the audience members you're

00:02:13.970 --> 00:02:21.230
hearing from, they trust you. Yes. And they
might trust you in part because of that transparency.

00:02:21.230 --> 00:02:26.840
And so overall, that that's a good thing.
But I think. An important sort of shift for

00:02:26.840 --> 00:02:35.160
us right now is to move away from this conflation
of objectivity or nonpartisanship or being

00:02:35.160 --> 00:02:42.050
non-biased with being trustworthy because
they are not inherently the same. And I think

00:02:42.050 --> 00:02:46.650
those concepts have become really muddled
in a way that's actually really damaging.

00:02:46.650 --> 00:02:51.200
Can you talk a little bit about objectivity
versus neutrality, which is sometimes a source

00:02:51.200 --> 00:02:58.230
of confusion? And the way I've talked about
it is I could be neutral on the issue of whether

00:02:58.230 --> 00:03:03.780
vaccines cause autism or I could be objective,
which is to transparently tell you that all

00:03:03.780 --> 00:03:08.510
the science tells us that they don't and that
that's the way I try to explain it. But there's

00:03:08.510 --> 00:03:11.760
probably a better umbrella way to talk about
that difference.

00:03:11.760 --> 00:03:24.601
Yeah, sure. So, you know, I am.
I am A.I. objectivity and neutrality, but

00:03:24.601 --> 00:03:32.540
I think that there is again, a way in which
those concepts have been conflated over time,

00:03:32.540 --> 00:03:39.790
where objectivity might be narrowly understood
as sort of what you're talking about.

00:03:39.790 --> 00:03:49.390
You know, rigorous kind of scientific approach
to analyzing the facts in an honest attempt

00:03:49.390 --> 00:03:56.730
to reflect the closest version of truth that
we can come up with. Right. And but more broadly,

00:03:56.730 --> 00:04:04.100
it's often been interpreted to also mean that
the reporter or the outlet is neutral on the

00:04:04.100 --> 00:04:11.510
issue or in some way impartial on the issue
or or stands outside of it in some way. And

00:04:11.510 --> 00:04:16.660
that's been sort of the journalism school
interpretation of objectivity as well for

00:04:16.660 --> 00:04:23.990
a long time, is that it's a reflection of
neutrality. For me, I think even just that,

00:04:23.990 --> 00:04:29.789
the idea that there is one objective truth
does kind of fall apart. You know, philosophically

00:04:29.789 --> 00:04:34.970
at a certain point and leaves us stuck with
some of the same issues that neutrality does

00:04:34.970 --> 00:04:40.010
of like how can we ever fully stand outside
of something and look at it objectively? And

00:04:40.010 --> 00:04:46.350
I don't think that that's possible, but I
do think that rigor, transparency, being meticulous

00:04:46.350 --> 00:04:52.080
and being, you know, striving for some version
of the truth is something that journalists

00:04:52.080 --> 00:04:57.750
can and should still embrace and that that
we do need to separate that out from some

00:04:57.750 --> 00:05:03.630
sort of performance of neutrality or performance
of impartiality that has been demanded of

00:05:03.630 --> 00:05:07.520
many journalists.
Yet the performative nature of neutrality

00:05:07.520 --> 00:05:11.661
is an interesting way to put it. And I think
maybe to think about that a little bit. One

00:05:11.661 --> 00:05:18.360
can visualize the sort of corporate legacy
media segments about, for example, climate

00:05:18.360 --> 00:05:23.800
change, where you have two guests, one of
two meetings, 50 percent represents the scientific

00:05:23.800 --> 00:05:28.572
view. One of two 50 percent represents some
other view. Right. China created it. It's

00:05:28.572 --> 00:05:33.790
a hoax or whatever that is. To me, that's
performative neutrality in the sense that

00:05:33.790 --> 00:05:41.700
it's 50/50 in this very basic sense, but it
does not reflect anything about what's actually

00:05:41.700 --> 00:05:47.060
happening out there in the scientific or factual
community. Why has corporate media decided

00:05:47.060 --> 00:05:52.490
to do that? Is it as simple as avoiding criticism
more from one political side than the other,

00:05:52.490 --> 00:05:59.140
or is there more to it?
So, yes, avoiding criticism is one piece.

00:05:59.140 --> 00:06:05.250
You know, I've heard people say that journalists,
for all that we are willing to go after other

00:06:05.250 --> 00:06:12.492
people, are some of the most thin skinned
folks around, which is neither here nor there

00:06:12.492 --> 00:06:17.140
to me. But but I think there are larger forces
at play.

00:06:17.140 --> 00:06:22.020
That has always been true in this sort of
performance of objectivity and neutrality,

00:06:22.020 --> 00:06:26.310
that it originated with a business model based
on advertising.

00:06:26.310 --> 00:06:32.730
So advertisers wanted to appeal to the largest
possible sort of general audience, but also

00:06:32.730 --> 00:06:36.900
often an audience that has money, that has
resources. Right. So that might have also

00:06:36.900 --> 00:06:41.780
translated or still translate to mean white
upper middle class audience.

00:06:41.780 --> 00:06:47.810
And so sort of striking a balance of perform
neutrality or performed impartiality that

00:06:47.810 --> 00:06:52.760
hits right at the middle of that target audience
is a priority for advertisers and underwriters

00:06:52.760 --> 00:06:59.080
and sponsors that certainly, you know, TV
news has taken extremely, extremely seriously

00:06:59.080 --> 00:07:04.190
this performance. But I also wouldn't underestimate,
you know, it's not pure economic forces.

00:07:04.190 --> 00:07:14.210
There are also political forces in particular
on the right that have worked very hard for

00:07:14.210 --> 00:07:22.840
going on five decades now to sort of frame
the concept of balance in terms of left, right,

00:07:22.840 --> 00:07:29.830
and then delineate a right wing perspective
that is actually very, very extreme as something

00:07:29.830 --> 00:07:34.330
normal and normative and demand that that
perspective be represented and quote unquote,

00:07:34.330 --> 00:07:39.490
balanced media. So the example that you gave
about climate change climate deniers are this

00:07:39.490 --> 00:07:44.760
tiny, tiny minority. Right. Certainly in the
scientific community, but also are a minority

00:07:44.760 --> 00:07:50.169
in the general populace in the United States.
And so the demand that they always be represented

00:07:50.169 --> 00:07:58.810
in a conversation about climate change is
a huge win for the sort of corporate right

00:07:58.810 --> 00:08:04.970
wing media machine that has pushed to make
that happen. And, you know, this sort of performance

00:08:04.970 --> 00:08:10.130
of objectivity, performance of balance and
the demand of that is so dangerous in the

00:08:10.130 --> 00:08:16.750
hands of demagogs. And that's what we're dealing
with now. Right. Is journalists sitting around

00:08:16.750 --> 00:08:21.940
talking about whether or not to say that Donald
Trump is lying because it might be perceived

00:08:21.940 --> 00:08:27.790
as partisan to say that he has lied when he
has lied. And you know what could be more

00:08:27.790 --> 00:08:34.690
toxic to the cause of truth than that interpretation
or that performance of objectivity and to

00:08:34.690 --> 00:08:38.740
have journalists in mainstream newsrooms sitting
around having that conversation, which they

00:08:38.740 --> 00:08:46.370
are, is such a huge win for far right activists
who want that to be the debate rather than

00:08:46.370 --> 00:08:49.330
the truth to be told. And then we debate the
real issues from there.

00:08:49.330 --> 00:08:53.980
Yeah, as a very brief aside, there's a great
book which may not even be in print anymore

00:08:53.980 --> 00:08:59.620
called The Waves of Rancor, which describes
a lot of the backstory on how that was built

00:08:59.620 --> 00:09:04.680
up initially. But so you mentioned this question
of do we say that Donald Trump is lying or

00:09:04.680 --> 00:09:09.960
do we not? Recently we've seen CNN doing some
sort of like fact checking graphically with

00:09:09.960 --> 00:09:13.710
notes at the bottom of the screen during the
Corona virus press briefings. Can you talk

00:09:13.710 --> 00:09:20.520
a little bit about the effect that the mainstreaming
of the term fake news as sort of as a cudgel

00:09:20.520 --> 00:09:28.150
has had on journalism and media?
Yeah, sure. So, you know, that mainstreaming

00:09:28.150 --> 00:09:35.690
of the term fake news and Donald Trump's sort
of use of it, as you said, as a as a cudgel,

00:09:35.690 --> 00:09:43.800
has been a powerful tool for advancing this
sort of warping of the debate.

00:09:43.800 --> 00:09:54.300
And this demand that every discussion be balanced
with with both far right ideology or in some

00:09:54.300 --> 00:10:02.700
cases now just Donald Trump's ideas, whatever
they may be. But that also that is part of

00:10:02.700 --> 00:10:10.440
a long standing tradition right in the in
the United States that has always been tied

00:10:10.440 --> 00:10:17.730
up with racism and white supremacy. And so
the example that I give in my book is this

00:10:17.730 --> 00:10:23.820
idea of the lost cause in the Civil War. And
so, you know, the civil war was at least in

00:10:23.820 --> 00:10:33.460
part about ending slavery, and slavery was
a terror on the people who were enslaved.

00:10:33.460 --> 00:10:38.640
You know, and I'm sure I don't need to tell
you that. But almost immediately after the

00:10:38.640 --> 00:10:43.730
end of the civil war, this sort of news story
was written about who these white southerners

00:10:43.730 --> 00:10:48.050
were who were fighting for the Confederacy
and what they were about, and that they weren't

00:10:48.050 --> 00:10:54.770
about slavery or enslavement at all. But,
you know, they just wanted states rights and

00:10:54.770 --> 00:10:58.180
their lost war.
Was this lost cause of of states rights that

00:10:58.180 --> 00:11:03.839
had nothing to do with racism. And and in
the late eighteen hundreds and early nineteen

00:11:03.839 --> 00:11:10.220
hundreds. Activists for this sort of lost
cause narrative demanded balance and textbooks.

00:11:10.220 --> 00:11:15.090
Right. And demanded that schools in favor
of not being less biased actually teach this

00:11:15.090 --> 00:11:20.170
story that you know, slavery had been this
sort of benign thing and that it wasn't really

00:11:20.170 --> 00:11:24.140
what Civil War was about. And these nice white
people just wanted their states rights. And,

00:11:24.140 --> 00:11:29.820
you know, and they were nice to the black
people anyway. And all this kind of B S, right.

00:11:29.820 --> 00:11:33.760
But that's like the original fake news like
this sort of you know, it's a lost cause and

00:11:33.760 --> 00:11:38.130
it's biased to teach it as something that
was actually about racism. And so that strategy

00:11:38.130 --> 00:11:42.400
has been used by racist demagogs in the United
States since forever. And that's just one

00:11:42.400 --> 00:11:47.690
example of it. Donald Trump is part of a long
tradition and, you know, unfortunately has

00:11:47.690 --> 00:11:53.500
amassed a great deal of power. But he's not
the first racist demagog to amass power.

00:11:53.500 --> 00:11:58.279
Certainly not really. So in the limited time
we have left, I would love to hear from you.

00:11:58.279 --> 00:12:02.910
What do you see as the lowest hanging fruit
of if we could start teaching media literacy

00:12:02.910 --> 00:12:06.660
in seventh grade, which I think would be a
great time to start teaching it? Three things

00:12:06.660 --> 00:12:12.110
I think would be really useful would be teaching.
Number one, understand the primary purpose

00:12:12.110 --> 00:12:15.650
of a news source, which would very quickly
get you to well, if the primary purpose is

00:12:15.650 --> 00:12:21.080
to make money, that needs to be included in
my analysis. Number two, understanding whether

00:12:21.080 --> 00:12:25.320
you're looking at a primary source or not.
And the number three, distinguishing between

00:12:25.320 --> 00:12:29.410
opinion journalism and news journalism, those
that's just an example. And there could be

00:12:29.410 --> 00:12:34.279
many other examples. What do you see if you
get a classroom of 12 year olds in front of

00:12:34.279 --> 00:12:38.970
you as the lowest hanging fruit that would
do the most good as far as media literacy

00:12:38.970 --> 00:12:44.720
goes?
Those are great. I love your three points.

00:12:44.720 --> 00:12:53.160
I think that my big takeaway not just for
children but for adults and this is great

00:12:53.160 --> 00:12:57.360
if we can start I mean, young is just curiosity,
right?

00:12:57.360 --> 00:13:02.710
That there's always another question. There's
always follow up. So I think we end up being

00:13:02.710 --> 00:13:06.280
fed this perception that what you read in
the news or what you hear on the news is sort

00:13:06.280 --> 00:13:14.750
of. That's it. That's the final story and
the myth of objectivity feeds into that. There

00:13:14.750 --> 00:13:18.800
are these objective people out there who are
journalists who just bring you the story and

00:13:18.800 --> 00:13:24.850
that's everything that you might need to know.
And either you trust them or you don't.

00:13:24.850 --> 00:13:31.810
But I think all of us right now really need
to be working our muscle of curiosity and

00:13:31.810 --> 00:13:37.240
of always asking more questions and investigating
further into things and essentially sort of

00:13:37.240 --> 00:13:43.810
become journalists in a way ourselves. Certainly
the crisis of Cauvin 19 and then pandemic

00:13:43.810 --> 00:13:48.230
has been an opportunity for that. People are
seeing in real time how journalistic fact

00:13:48.230 --> 00:13:54.050
and truth actually isn't set in stone. It
changes pretty quickly in an environment where

00:13:54.050 --> 00:13:58.130
even scientists don't know what this virus
is. Right. And so all of our ability to be

00:13:58.130 --> 00:14:02.870
curious and open minded and to actually change
the way that we think about something based

00:14:02.870 --> 00:14:08.589
on new information and ask new questions over
time, to me, that's sort of the core for both

00:14:08.589 --> 00:14:13.899
people taking in media and for people producing
it is that we can all embody more of that

00:14:13.899 --> 00:14:18.510
curious journalists or curious scientists
and that a scientist or a journalist. The

00:14:18.510 --> 00:14:23.250
point of that person is not that they have
the best answers, but that they have the best

00:14:23.250 --> 00:14:27.030
questions. And I think that's often misunderstood
and process is important.

00:14:27.030 --> 00:14:30.300
Right, understanding that because something
that was said about the virus six weeks ago

00:14:30.300 --> 00:14:35.070
ended up not being true doesn't mean someone
was necessarily lying. It could mean that.

00:14:35.070 --> 00:14:39.700
But we are sort of iterating. Science is an
iterative process. So is journalism and understanding

00:14:39.700 --> 00:14:44.800
that because that's also weaponized sometimes
to say the thing they told us six weeks ago

00:14:44.800 --> 00:14:51.290
was wrong. Therefore, Bill Gates is trying
to get a vaccine to sterilize people or whatever.

00:14:51.290 --> 00:14:56.700
It's all on the table because something was
corrected. The process part seems so important.

00:14:56.700 --> 00:15:05.680
Yeah, and understanding that process of investigation
and inquisitiveness, and I think that's one

00:15:05.680 --> 00:15:10.680
of the most important things that we could
be teaching in terms of media literacy, is

00:15:10.680 --> 00:15:18.180
that that's all that news is. Right. Is the
best attempt at providing facts and information

00:15:18.180 --> 00:15:22.290
of a group of people who brought it to you
today.

00:15:22.290 --> 00:15:27.000
We've been speaking with Lewis Raven Wallace,
author and creator of The View From Somewhere.

00:15:27.000 --> 00:15:32.970
A book and podcast about the myth of objectivity
in journalism. So great having you on. I really

00:15:32.970 --> 00:15:35.980
appreciate your time.
Thank you for having me.

